WEBVTT

1
00:00:07.841 --> 00:00:12.926
Tom Lindqvist: Hey Dale, hey Ryan. Thanks for making the time. How's Sioux Falls treating you today?

2
00:00:14.146 --> 00:00:18.384
Dale Henderson: Cold, but we'll take it. Good to see you, Tom.

3
00:00:19.195 --> 00:00:25.541
Ryan Fischer: Yeah, busy week. We're in the middle of some dev work, so forgive me if I'm half here.

4
00:00:26.146 --> 00:00:36.430
Tom Lindqvist: No problem. I want to walk through the numbers first, then talk about the agent, and leave ten minutes for anything you want to flag.

5
00:00:37.769 --> 00:00:38.952
Dale Henderson: Works for us.

6
00:00:39.412 --> 00:00:48.890
Tom Lindqvist: So quarter over quarter, tracked calls are up 11 percent. About 9,400 last month, Google Ads driving a bit over half of that.

7
00:00:50.232 --> 00:00:57.708
Dale Henderson: That tracks with what we're seeing on the media side. Google's been pushing more of the fiber campaigns.

8
00:00:58.097 --> 00:01:06.283
Tom Lindqvist: And the AI Voice Agent, outage calls are holding around 800 a week. That's pretty consistent since the summer.

9
00:01:07.243 --> 00:01:15.454
Ryan Fischer: Yeah, that number's been steady. After the storm in August, we just left the configuration where it was and never went back.

10
00:01:16.720 --> 00:01:20.456
Tom Lindqvist: Makes sense. How's staffing on the contact center side?

11
00:01:20.718 --> 00:01:29.857
Dale Henderson: We lost a rep in Rapid City last month, so we're running two short while we hire. Nadine's covering some of the overnight shift.

12
00:01:30.204 --> 00:01:35.498
Tom Lindqvist: Got it. Anything you want me to look at while you're short-handed?

13
00:01:36.885 --> 00:01:42.412
Dale Henderson: Not right now. Actually, the agent's been taking some of the pressure off.

14
00:01:43.867 --> 00:01:49.649
Tom Lindqvist: Good to hear. Ryan, you mentioned you wanted to talk about something on the web side?

15
00:01:50.752 --> 00:01:58.232
Ryan Fischer: Yeah. So we rebuilt the order flow as a single-page app back in September. Everything's client-side routed now.

16
00:01:59.069 --> 00:02:01.463
Tom Lindqvist: Okay. And what are you seeing?

17
00:02:02.449 --> 00:02:12.312
Ryan Fischer: The dynamic number insertion only fires on that first page load. If somebody clicks through to the second step of the flow, the number on the page stops swapping.

18
00:02:13.112 --> 00:02:17.082
Tom Lindqvist: So the visitor's still seeing the tracking number from the entry page?

19
00:02:18.502 --> 00:02:31.063
Ryan Fischer: Right. Which is fine if they came in on the same campaign, but if they land on one ad and then click a different offer, we're attributing the call to the wrong thing.

20
00:02:31.889 --> 00:02:40.907
Dale Henderson: It's not breaking anything, but it's showing up in the reporting. We had a couple of fiber campaigns cross-attributing last month.

21
00:02:41.530 --> 00:02:44.942
Tom Lindqvist: How many calls are we talking about? Ballpark.

22
00:02:46.356 --> 00:02:55.311
Ryan Fischer: Maybe five, six percent of the calls that come through the order flow. Enough that I don't want to leave it.

23
00:02:55.813 --> 00:03:01.397
Tom Lindqvist: Okay. So the route changes are happening after the tag's already run, and it's not re-evaluating.

24
00:03:01.898 --> 00:03:07.985
Ryan Fischer: That's my read. I can send you the test page and the steps to reproduce.

25
00:03:09.277 --> 00:03:18.935
Tom Lindqvist: Please do. I'll take it to our tracking team and see what they say. I don't want to guess at it on the call.

26
00:03:20.432 --> 00:03:24.486
Ryan Fischer: Appreciate it. I'll have it over to you by tomorrow.

27
00:03:25.677 --> 00:03:35.180
Dale Henderson: And just so we're clear, it's not urgent. It's a medium thing for us. We just want clean attribution before the spring campaigns.

28
00:03:36.222 --> 00:03:42.450
Tom Lindqvist: Understood. Let me note the timeline. Spring is when you start pushing the new markets, right?

29
00:03:43.291 --> 00:03:48.381
Dale Henderson: Yeah, we're looking at two new markets in Nebraska and Kansas. Probably March.

30
00:03:48.727 --> 00:03:52.883
Tom Lindqvist: Okay, that gives us some room. Anything else on your list?

31
00:03:53.608 --> 00:03:59.778
Dale Henderson: Not really. Marketo's been fine. We're not doing anything new with it this quarter.

32
00:04:00.839 --> 00:04:09.847
Tom Lindqvist: Good. I'll check in on the tracking item as soon as I hear something. Do you want a follow-up call, or just email?

33
00:04:11.347 --> 00:04:15.604
Dale Henderson: Email's fine. Ryan's the one who'll care about the details.

34
00:04:16.102 --> 00:04:17.981
Ryan Fischer: Yeah, just loop me in.

35
00:04:18.997 --> 00:04:28.342
Tom Lindqvist: Will do. One last thing: your renewal's next October, so we've got time, but I'll start pulling together a review for the spring.

36
00:04:28.576 --> 00:04:32.921
Dale Henderson: Sounds good. Hey, random question, did you ever get that camping trip in?

37
00:04:34.250 --> 00:04:40.507
Tom Lindqvist: Twice, actually. The Badlands in June and then a quick one up by Lead in August.

38
00:04:41.540 --> 00:04:47.270
Dale Henderson: Nice. We did the Black Hills over Labor Day. Took the kids, it rained the whole time.

39
00:04:47.659 --> 00:04:48.459
Ryan Fischer: Every time.

40
00:04:49.793 --> 00:04:56.166
Tom Lindqvist: Ha. Okay, I'll send the notes and a summary by end of day tomorrow. Thanks again, both of you.

41
00:04:56.709 --> 00:04:58.215
Dale Henderson: Thanks, Tom. Talk soon.

42
00:04:59.498 --> 00:05:00.614
Ryan Fischer: Yep, take care.
